{
  "name": "vrp_spx",
  "ref": "repro:vrp_spx",
  "built": "2026-10-24",
  "claim": "Implied volatility overstates realized volatility most of the time, so selling premium has positive expected value",
  "tasty_value": "IV often overestimates realized volatility, a cushion that pays option sellers (no figure given)",
  "tasty_sources": [
    "page:concepts-implied-vs-realized-volatility",
    "page:concepts-how-to-start-trading-options-entry-checklist"
  ],
  "tasty_note": "Filled in Phase 02 (2026-10-25) from tastylive pages and checked episode extracts.",
  "our_value": "VIX exceeded the next 21 days' realized vol on 85% of days; mean spread +4.1 vol pts (median +4.7); realized averaged 0.78x VIX. Years with a negative mean spread: 2008.",
  "data": "Yahoo ^VIX close and ^GSPC close (daily)",
  "period": "1990-01-02 to 2026-09-24",
  "method": "For each day: VIX/100 vs the annualized standard deviation of the next 21 trading days' SPX close-to-close returns (zenith.beta.hedge.realized). IV rank = VIX position in its trailing 252-day high-low range. Days overlap, so n overstates independent observations about 21-fold.",
  "caveats": [
    "VIX is a 30-calendar-day variance-swap rate and is quoted as vol; comparing it to 21-day realized standard deviation is the usual approximation and slightly flatters the spread (Jensen gap between variance and vol).",
    "A positive vol spread is not the seller's P&L: losses scale with gamma and jumps, so a short option can lose in a month where the average spread was positive.",
    "Index only; single-stock premia are smaller (lit:carr-wu-2009).",
    "IV-rank buckets are not a tradable test of IVR timing: high-IVR days cluster in a few episodes."
  ],
  "verdict": "agrees",
